131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Below, it should be more clear how the motor is driven with 9V, yet is controlled with 5V. This is because it has modifications from a normal H-Bridge.  The transistors are named clockwise starting from the left middle  Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4. Q1 and Q2 are PNP transistors, and Q3 and Q4 are NPN. The two transistors on the far left and right are named Q5 and Q6 respectively, and are also NPN transistors.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 114 0 R
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 114 0 R
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
134 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (When the Arduino outputs a high PWM signal, Q1 saturates, because in NPN transistors, if the voltage between its base and emitter will typically saturate at a V_be >= 0.7V, of which its emitter is grounded, thus 5V-0V > 0.7V. This means that the voltage drop across it is almost 0V, and therefore, the voltage at the base of Q1 is 0V. Because Q1 is a PNP transistor, and it saturates at a difference between its  V_eb at <= 0.7V, and its emitter is at 9V and its base is at 9V, then the voltage drop across it is also amost 0, and the voltage supplied to the motor is the full 9V.)
/Pg 8 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
